===Etymology 2===
From {{der|en|enm|bark}}, from {{der|en|ang|barc||bark}}, from {{der|en|non|bǫrkr||tree bark}}, from {{der|en|gem-pro|*barkuz}}, probably related to {{m|gem-pro|*birkijǭ||birch}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*bʰerHǵós}} (compare {{cog|la|frāxinus||ash}}, {{cog|lt|béržas||birch}}), from Proto-Indo-European {{m|ine-pro|*bʰerHǵ-||to gleam; white}} (compare {{cog|en|bright}}); akin to {{cog|da|bark}}, {{cog|is|börkur}}, {{cog|nds|borke}} and {{cog|sq|berk||bast}}.

====Noun====
{{wp}}
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{senseid|en|Q184453}} {{lb|en|countable|uncountable}} The exterior covering of the trunk and branches of a [[tree]] or of various other [[woody]] plants.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1879|author=Friedrich August Flückiger; et al.|title=Pharmacographia...|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=iTTeAgAAQBAJ&pg=PA346|page=346|passage=The hardships of [[bark]]-collecting in the primeval forests of South America are of the severest kind, and undergone only by the half-civilized [[Indian]]s and people of [[mixed race]], in the pay of speculators or companies located in the towns. Those who are engaged in the business, especially the collectors themselves, are called ''[[cascarillero|Cascarilleros]]'' or ''[[cascador|Cascadores]]'', from the Spanish word ''[[cáscara|Cascara]]'', [[bark]].}}
#* {{RQ:NYT|date=26 December 2012|author=[[w:John Branch (journalist)|John Branch]]|title=Snow Fall: The Avalanche at Tunnel Creek|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20131114133825/http://www.nytimes.com/projects/2012/snow-fall/?smid=fb-nytimes&WT.z_sma=SP_SNT_20130415#/?part=tunnel-creek|passage=Moving about 70 miles per hour, it crashed through the sturdy old-growth trees, snapping their limbs and shredding '''bark''' from their trunks.}}
# {{lb|en|medicine}} [[Peruvian bark]] or [[Jesuit's bark]], the bark of the [[cinchona]] from which [[quinine]] is produced.
# {{senseid|en|candy}} Hard [[candy]] made in flat sheets, for instance out of chocolate, peanut butter, toffee or peppermint.
# {{senseid|en|crust}} The [[crust]] formed on [[barbecue]]d [[meat]] that has had a [[rub]] applied to it.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2009|author=Julie Reinhardt|title=She-Smoke: A Backyard Barbecue Book|page=151|passage=This softens the meat further, but at some loss of crunch to the '''bark'''.}}
# The envelopment or outer covering of anything.
# {{lb|en|dialectical|Western US}} [[woodchip|Woodchips]].

