#* {{RQ:Irving Columbus|volume=II|chapter=Voyage through the Gulf of Paria|pages=169–170|pageref=169|passage=They treated them with profound reverence, as beings descended from heaven, and conducted them to a spacious house, the residence of the cacique, where they were '''banquetted''' in their simple but hospitable way, with bread and various fruits of excellent flavour, and different kinds of beverages which have been already mentioned.}}

